WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.480
Araya
Maruo

00:00:02.390 --> 00:00:04.480
<i>I'll do something about it, no matter what!</i>

00:00:07.900 --> 00:00:10.430
<i>I was able to get that far during a return game.</i>

00:00:10.430 --> 00:00:12.490
<i>I'll be able to hold my next serve!</i>

00:00:13.160 --> 00:00:16.240
<i>I was able to prevent 
myself from getting worse!</i>

00:00:17.490 --> 00:00:19.240
<i>I don't understand.</i>

00:00:19.710 --> 00:00:23.000
<i>This is the final round of the 
high-level Kanagawa tournament.</i>

00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:24.940
<i>Why would someone who made it this far</i>

00:00:24.940 --> 00:00:27.570
<i>be so stymied by a left-handed player?</i>

00:00:28.730 --> 00:00:29.690
<i>No...</i>

00:00:29.690 --> 00:00:31.260
<i>He was like that, too.</i>

00:00:31.670 --> 00:00:33.630
<i>Although it's not as much as Ike,</i>

00:00:33.630 --> 00:00:36.760
<i>I can't underestimate your speed of growth.</i>

00:00:37.210 --> 00:00:39.990
<i>I'll have to do something about 
that as soon as possible.</i>

00:02:12.330 --> 00:02:16.320
Choices and Explosive Power

00:02:20.110 --> 00:02:24.120
First set winner: Takasaki. Score: 6-2.

00:02:24.120 --> 00:02:25.250
Yes!

00:02:25.250 --> 00:02:27.300
Takasaki Natsu's really strong!

00:02:27.300 --> 00:02:28.520
And cute, too!

00:02:28.520 --> 00:02:31.790
She's in the nation's top four, 
so she's bound to be great in Kanazawa.

00:02:30.880 --> 00:02:35.130
Araya
Maruo

00:02:31.790 --> 00:02:34.200
Come to think of it, I wonder
how the boys' side is doing.

00:02:35.480 --> 00:02:36.210
Time.

00:02:36.580 --> 00:02:40.420
<i>For now, I've figured out how to 
deal with a left-handed player.</i>

00:02:40.420 --> 00:02:43.220
<i>But in my current state, 
this is the best I can do.</i>

00:02:43.630 --> 00:02:45.480
<i>I have to hold this next serve.</i>

00:02:45.480 --> 00:02:47.220
<i>Aim mainly toward his backhand!</i>

00:02:48.250 --> 00:02:51.230
<i>I may not be used to his left backhand,</i>

00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:53.230
<i>but I'll make this work!</i>

00:02:55.440 --> 00:02:56.800
<i>Even if I mainly aim for his backhand,</i>

00:02:56.800 --> 00:02:58.820
<i>I can still scope out the right time for a...</i>

00:02:58.820 --> 00:02:59.490
<i>Forehand!</i>

00:02:59.900 --> 00:03:02.950
<i>I want to move up, but if I can't do that,</i>

00:03:03.240 --> 00:03:05.280
<i>I'll watch for his left forehand crosscourt!</i>

00:03:05.910 --> 00:03:06.290
<i>That's it!</i>

00:03:06.580 --> 00:03:07.960
<i>Forehand crosscourt!</i>

00:03:07.960 --> 00:03:09.790
<i>The ball reaches farther 
than his right backhand!</i>

00:03:10.290 --> 00:03:11.390
<i>Until I get used to it,</i>

00:03:11.390 --> 00:03:14.050
<i>I'll play it safe and use my 1/36 control!</i>

00:03:16.450 --> 00:03:18.050
Out. 15-love.

00:03:18.510 --> 00:03:21.510
<i>Looks like he's used to my left-handed play.</i>

00:03:21.510 --> 00:03:23.020
<i>Even if it was his mistake,</i>

00:03:23.020 --> 00:03:25.180
<i>I scored by returning his 
forehand with my backhand!</i>

00:03:25.180 --> 00:03:25.850
<i>I can do this!</i>

00:03:25.850 --> 00:03:26.920
Araya, don't worry about it!

00:03:26.920 --> 00:03:28.550
Keep attacking during the return game!

00:03:28.550 --> 00:03:30.140
Way to hang in there, Ei-chan!

00:03:32.150 --> 00:03:33.450
<i>That's right.</i>

00:03:33.450 --> 00:03:37.240
<i>You've got no choice but to come 
up with a safe strategy for now.</i>

00:03:37.240 --> 00:03:40.950
<i>All the while trying to avoid 
being trumped by his backhand.</i>

00:03:40.950 --> 00:03:42.990
Their strokes are getting longer.

00:03:42.990 --> 00:03:44.910
Yeah, Maruo's keeping up, too.

00:03:44.910 --> 00:03:47.720
<i>If I can get an edge on him while it's my serve,</i>

00:03:47.720 --> 00:03:50.250
<i>I can figure out how to deal 
with left-handed play</i>

00:03:50.250 --> 00:03:52.330
<i>by testing out different safe strategies.</i>

00:03:55.930 --> 00:03:57.610
<i>Learn it with my body,</i>

00:03:57.610 --> 00:04:00.140
<i>and increase the accuracy of 
my control little by little.</i>

00:04:00.820 --> 00:04:03.070
<i>By taking technical misses 
out of the equation,</i>

00:04:03.070 --> 00:04:04.590
<i>I'll be less likely to lose confidence.</i>

00:04:06.850 --> 00:04:08.850
Game: Maruo. 1-0.

00:04:08.850 --> 00:04:09.750
Yes!

00:04:09.750 --> 00:04:10.550
I held!

00:04:10.550 --> 00:04:11.950
Nice job, Ei-chan!

00:04:11.950 --> 00:04:14.770
Yeah, it looks like he's bouncing back.

00:04:14.770 --> 00:04:16.440
You can do it, Maruo-kun!

00:04:16.870 --> 00:04:18.520
<i>No, not yet.</i>

00:04:18.520 --> 00:04:20.940
<i>All I did was hold out.</i>

00:04:21.270 --> 00:04:24.200
<i>I haven't gotten up to being able 
to break in a return game.</i>

00:04:24.610 --> 00:04:26.580
<i>I learned during the first set that</i>

00:04:26.580 --> 00:04:28.820
<i>I should disrupt the tempo or 
do something unexpected</i>

00:04:28.820 --> 00:04:32.250
<i>when facing someone who 
has both speed and power.</i>

00:04:32.680 --> 00:04:34.410
<i>Also, that he's left-handed,</i>

00:04:34.410 --> 00:04:36.420
<i>and that his serves are especially intense.</i>

00:04:37.000 --> 00:04:40.200
<i>Which means how I return 
this serve and surprise him</i>

00:04:40.200 --> 00:04:41.480
<i>is what will lead to my break!</i>

00:04:42.580 --> 00:04:44.920
<i>A step and a half out, 
forward two ball widths!</i>

00:04:46.690 --> 00:04:47.930
<i>It's too fast to get to in time.</i>

00:04:48.480 --> 00:04:49.430
<i>But it went in!</i>

00:04:51.290 --> 00:04:52.430
Love-15.

00:04:52.430 --> 00:04:53.270
Huh?

00:04:55.450 --> 00:04:57.050
<i>What was that error just now?</i>

00:04:57.520 --> 00:05:01.070
<i>I barely returned that ball. 
It was his chance to win the point.</i>

00:05:01.590 --> 00:05:04.930
<i>Maybe his timing was off 
because I swung harder?</i>

00:05:05.820 --> 00:05:07.890
<i>If that's the case, does it mean I was able</i>

00:05:07.890 --> 00:05:09.830
<i>to surprise him, even if it was accidental?</i>

00:05:10.380 --> 00:05:13.560
<i>I knew that doing something 
unexpected would be effective,</i>

00:05:13.560 --> 00:05:16.240
<i>but I was too occupied with how 
to deal with his left-handed play.</i>

00:05:16.240 --> 00:05:18.750
<i>But I have to break next.</i>

00:05:18.750 --> 00:05:22.280
<i>How can I surprise him to 
neutralize his speed and power</i>

00:05:22.280 --> 00:05:23.340
<i>so I can break?</i>

00:05:24.440 --> 00:05:25.840
<i>A slice that tails outwards!</i>

00:05:26.710 --> 00:05:27.880
<i>A step and a half out.</i>

00:05:27.880 --> 00:05:29.850
<i>Imagine three ball widths 
ahead of where it is!</i>

00:05:31.100 --> 00:05:32.560
<i>I sent it back somehow!</i>

00:05:33.310 --> 00:05:35.270
<i>This forehand is a beat faster.</i>

00:05:35.270 --> 00:05:37.810
<i>Until I get used to it, 1/36!</i>

00:05:39.260 --> 00:05:39.810
<i>Yes!</i>

00:05:40.510 --> 00:05:41.940
<i>Now throw him off!</i>

00:05:46.610 --> 00:05:47.570
<i>He noticed!</i>

00:05:50.500 --> 00:05:51.780
15-all.

00:05:52.170 --> 00:05:53.120
Yeah!

00:05:53.450 --> 00:05:54.690
<i>He got me!</i>

00:05:54.690 --> 00:05:58.060
<i>The change in the ball's speed from that 
last point made him more aware of it!</i>

00:05:58.500 --> 00:06:03.130
<i>A drop shot may not work until I'm 
in a more advantageous position.</i>

00:06:03.890 --> 00:06:06.060
<i>How do I gain the advantage?</i>

00:06:12.230 --> 00:06:14.180
Out. 30-15.

00:06:14.180 --> 00:06:17.300
Huh? He returned it full 
force all of a sudden.

00:06:17.300 --> 00:06:19.020
That had a bit too much power to it.

00:06:19.280 --> 00:06:22.150
But you can't take on Araya 
without that kind of force.

00:06:22.540 --> 00:06:24.600
<i>A break is always risky.</i>

00:06:24.600 --> 00:06:27.770
<i>I have to switch gears and gain the upper hand,</i>

00:06:27.770 --> 00:06:29.540
<i>even if it's just for a second.</i>

00:06:29.950 --> 00:06:33.330
<i>It's impossible to surprise someone who's
so aware of changes in the ball's speed.</i>

00:06:34.460 --> 00:06:37.660
<i>A ad court slice goes about a step 
and a half outside the sideline.</i>

00:06:39.190 --> 00:06:40.500
40-15.

00:06:41.910 --> 00:06:44.400
<i>He's starting to change up his trajectories.</i>

00:06:44.400 --> 00:06:47.430
<i>That's right. Araya-kun can 
change directions with his wrists,</i>

00:06:47.430 --> 00:06:49.510
<i>even if it's right before making contact.</i>

00:06:51.450 --> 00:06:52.740
<i>Watch closely.</i>

00:06:52.740 --> 00:06:54.190
<i>Prepare in advance.</i>

00:06:55.670 --> 00:06:57.220
<i>Hit it with all I've got!</i>

00:06:57.220 --> 00:06:58.010
<i>It went in!</i>

00:06:58.500 --> 00:06:59.960
<i>The court's open now!</i>

00:06:59.960 --> 00:07:02.810
<i>I have less risk in this moment, 
which gives me the advantage!</i>

00:07:03.060 --> 00:07:03.650
<i>And now...</i>

00:07:03.650 --> 00:07:05.320
<i>Throw him off!</i>

00:07:16.070 --> 00:07:17.330
40-30.

00:07:17.330 --> 00:07:18.450
Yes!

00:07:18.450 --> 00:07:21.250
Wow! That was a great drop shot!

00:07:21.250 --> 00:07:23.490
Araya couldn't get to it. 
Isn't that unbelievable?

00:07:23.490 --> 00:07:25.170
It was really well-controlled.

00:07:25.580 --> 00:07:29.850
But wouldn't he have won that 
point even without a drop shot?

00:07:29.850 --> 00:07:31.380
Probably.

00:07:31.380 --> 00:07:35.100
But that's exactly when you 
should hit a drop shot.

00:07:35.100 --> 00:07:38.910
Until now, Maruo has been returning 
those shots crosscourt and running up.

00:07:38.910 --> 00:07:40.540
Because those have been successful,

00:07:40.540 --> 00:07:42.940
Araya got there a step too late.

00:07:42.940 --> 00:07:46.680
It's a big breakthrough for him to 
make the choice to play offensively.

00:07:47.160 --> 00:07:48.190
<i>Huh...</i>

00:07:48.600 --> 00:07:50.600
<i>That was unexpected.</i>

00:07:51.370 --> 00:07:54.570
<i>Are you planning to increase
the variety of your attack patterns?</i>

00:07:55.410 --> 00:07:56.910
<i>Give it a try.</i>

00:07:57.170 --> 00:07:59.850
<i>I never questioned it in the past,</i>

00:07:59.850 --> 00:08:01.610
<i>but I didn't know how many ways there are</i>

00:08:01.610 --> 00:08:03.870
<i>to win points when you have 
the upper hand in a match.</i>

00:08:05.100 --> 00:08:08.130
<i>That point just now may have been a big deal!</i>

00:08:08.540 --> 00:08:13.200
<i>Okay. First, I have to find a way to return 
wide serves that put me at a disadvantage</i>

00:08:13.200 --> 00:08:15.190
<i>so that I can turn it to my advantage.</i>

00:08:15.030 --> 00:08:20.680
{left column} Wide          Step and a half

00:08:15.190 --> 00:08:17.460
<i>I can get to the wide shot in a step and a half.</i>

00:08:17.460 --> 00:08:18.520
<i>But if I'm too fast,</i>

00:08:18.520 --> 00:08:20.680
<i>he'll switch his aim to the center.</i>

00:08:18.660 --> 00:08:20.680
Center

00:08:20.680 --> 00:08:23.270
<i>Then first, I'll...</i>

00:08:23.520 --> 00:08:24.210
Huh?

00:08:24.210 --> 00:08:27.080
Ei-chan's farther to the side than usual.

00:08:27.080 --> 00:08:28.460
Is that okay?

00:08:28.460 --> 00:08:31.100
Yeah, I think that's fine.

00:08:31.100 --> 00:08:32.840
What, really?

00:08:32.840 --> 00:08:37.030
Yeah, it's a pretty common way of 
dealing with a left-handed serve.

00:08:37.030 --> 00:08:38.240
Just watch.

00:08:38.240 --> 00:08:40.240
<i>How's this?</i>

00:08:40.240 --> 00:08:41.520
Hard to hit          Normal position

00:08:40.240 --> 00:08:53.260
Wide slice

00:08:40.660 --> 00:08:43.180
<i>If I stay on the left side of court as usual,</i>

00:08:41.640 --> 00:08:41.770
Hard to hit          Normal position

00:08:41.890 --> 00:08:42.020
Hard to hit          Normal position

00:08:42.140 --> 00:08:42.270
Hard to hit          Normal position

00:08:42.390 --> 00:08:47.770
Hard to hit          Normal position

00:08:43.180 --> 00:08:45.360
<i>it's hard to hit a slice that goes out wide,</i>

00:08:45.360 --> 00:08:47.480
<i>a shot typical of left-handed players.</i>

00:08:47.770 --> 00:08:50.770
Difficult, but...

00:08:48.470 --> 00:08:50.700
<i>I know it'll be difficult to get to the center,</i>

00:08:50.700 --> 00:08:53.260
<i>but here, I don't have to worry about wide shots.</i>

00:08:50.900 --> 00:08:51.030
Hard to hit          Normal position

00:08:51.150 --> 00:08:53.260
Hard to hit                Normal position

00:08:53.530 --> 00:08:56.010
<i>In order to have the upper 
hand in a return game,</i>

00:08:56.010 --> 00:08:58.050
<i>I have to stop this serve first!</i>

00:08:58.050 --> 00:09:00.910
<i>Just minutes ago, he was baffled
by my being left-handed,</i>

00:09:00.910 --> 00:09:02.610
<i>and he's come this far already?</i>

00:09:02.610 --> 00:09:03.790
<i>That's fine.</i>

00:09:03.790 --> 00:09:08.170
<i>I'm already used to petty tricks like that!</i>

00:09:09.730 --> 00:09:10.590
Fault!

00:09:10.590 --> 00:09:12.500
Whoa, that was so close!

00:09:12.500 --> 00:09:15.200
If the center's wide open like that,

00:09:15.200 --> 00:09:17.440
he'll obviously go for it.

00:09:18.020 --> 00:09:22.040
But the zone for an ace is narrow 
for a serve down the center.

00:09:22.040 --> 00:09:27.420
Wide 
(Front of opponent)

00:09:22.040 --> 00:09:27.420
Center (Easy to fault)

00:09:22.400 --> 00:09:24.580
That being said, I can't say that

00:09:24.580 --> 00:09:27.420
boldly aiming wide is a good plan.

00:09:27.420 --> 00:09:28.900
I see.

00:09:28.900 --> 00:09:31.000
It ends up being difficult 
to aim for either target.

00:09:31.280 --> 00:09:34.820
Of course, Araya knows this already.

00:09:34.820 --> 00:09:38.010
He probably chose to aim for the 
center after considering his options.

00:09:38.010 --> 00:09:41.410
But that was a big asset for Maruo.

00:09:41.410 --> 00:09:44.150
Now Araya has to give a lot 
more thought to something

00:09:44.150 --> 00:09:47.350
he used to be able to do without thinking.

00:09:47.940 --> 00:09:50.580
<i>That was dangerous because it was flat,</i>

00:09:50.580 --> 00:09:54.200
<i>so the second serve will 
probably be safer, like a slice.</i>

00:09:54.740 --> 00:09:57.360
<i>A slice would be slower, too, 
so I can deal with it.</i>

00:09:58.120 --> 00:10:00.780
<i>If this gets to deuce, 
I can go for the break!</i>

00:10:01.740 --> 00:10:03.570
<i>Good! A wide slice!</i>

00:10:03.570 --> 00:10:06.430
<i>Since I have more leeway in 
this position, I can do it!</i>

00:10:07.930 --> 00:10:08.960
He's running to the net!

00:10:08.960 --> 00:10:10.320
It's a great return, too!

00:10:10.320 --> 00:10:11.590
<i>Now, bring it! Counter!</i>

00:10:11.590 --> 00:10:14.340
Forehand

00:10:11.590 --> 00:10:14.340
Cross

00:10:13.240 --> 00:10:14.340
<i>Cross!</i>

00:10:22.350 --> 00:10:23.100
<i>Damn!</i>

00:10:24.510 --> 00:10:25.100
Deuce!

00:10:25.450 --> 00:10:27.660
<i>I barely touched it, and I hit a winner!</i>

00:10:27.660 --> 00:10:31.190
<i>He hit it so low, all I could 
do was hit a deep return...</i>

00:10:31.880 --> 00:10:34.430
<i>That drop shot earlier really did work.</i>

00:10:34.870 --> 00:10:38.780
<i>The time it took him to decide 
which attack to use delayed him.</i>

00:10:40.010 --> 00:10:40.910
<i>Okay!</i>

00:10:40.910 --> 00:10:43.120
<i>Only two more points until a break!</i>

00:10:43.440 --> 00:10:45.930
<i>I'll return it toward the
deuce court with all I've got.</i>

00:10:45.930 --> 00:10:49.900
<i>His serve is fast, so I won't need 
a huge swing or a lot of power.</i>

00:10:49.900 --> 00:10:52.130
<i>Imagine hitting the ball with my weight.</i>

00:10:52.570 --> 00:10:53.900
<i>It's all about timing!</i>

00:10:56.660 --> 00:10:57.380
It cut in at him!

00:11:00.010 --> 00:11:01.390
It's in!

00:11:02.610 --> 00:11:04.410
Advantage: Maruo!

00:11:04.410 --> 00:11:05.640
Yes!

00:11:11.140 --> 00:11:12.650
Game, set, and match.

00:11:12.650 --> 00:11:16.650
Winner: Takasaki. Score: 6-2, 6-0.

00:11:16.650 --> 00:11:19.350
Takasaki Natsu's won the Kanagawa 
tournament two years in a row!

00:11:19.350 --> 00:11:20.560
And she's cute!

00:11:20.560 --> 00:11:22.280
She did it! Congratulations!

00:11:22.700 --> 00:11:25.030
Now Maruo's got break point.

00:11:25.030 --> 00:11:27.120
It's not like Araya's doing poorly.

00:11:27.120 --> 00:11:29.040
The game's just in Maruo's favor right now.

00:11:32.330 --> 00:11:33.860
Good job, Ei-chan!

00:11:33.860 --> 00:11:36.890
Maybe luck's been on his side since 
the ball that hit the net went in.

00:11:36.890 --> 00:11:39.330
It went in because he really went for it.

00:11:39.330 --> 00:11:40.630
It's not just luck.

00:11:40.630 --> 00:11:42.690
<i>If I can do this, I can break!</i>

00:11:42.690 --> 00:11:45.250
<i>I have to concentrate on the first serve here.</i>

00:11:46.520 --> 00:11:47.390
<i>That means...</i>

00:11:50.180 --> 00:11:50.770
<i>Wide!</i>

00:11:50.770 --> 00:11:51.770
<i>It hit!</i>

00:11:52.090 --> 00:11:53.770
<i>If I have the upper hand, then...</i>

00:11:53.770 --> 00:11:54.900
Another drop shot!

00:11:59.850 --> 00:12:01.450
Game: Maruo. 2-0.

00:12:01.450 --> 00:12:02.400
Second set.

00:12:02.400 --> 00:12:03.530
<i>I got it!</i>

00:12:03.530 --> 00:12:05.220
Yes!

00:12:05.220 --> 00:12:06.450
Nice, Bro!

00:12:06.450 --> 00:12:08.870
He finally broke Araya's serve!

00:12:09.790 --> 00:12:11.150
Seriously?

00:12:11.560 --> 00:12:12.960
Maruo's awesome.

00:12:12.960 --> 00:12:15.150
<i>Yes... Yes!</i>

00:12:15.150 --> 00:12:17.410
<i>My first break in this match!</i>

00:12:17.880 --> 00:12:18.600
<i>Huh?</i>

00:12:18.600 --> 00:12:22.120
<i>N-No, it's still the beginning of the 
second set, and it's just one break!</i>

00:12:22.120 --> 00:12:23.180
<i>Pull it together!</i>

00:12:23.780 --> 00:12:28.930
<i>Since I lost the first set, I have 
to win this set, no matter what.</i>

00:12:28.930 --> 00:12:33.190
<i>And yet, I'm still handling the left 
crosscourt shot cautiously.</i>

00:12:33.730 --> 00:12:38.460
<i>But now I know that if I have the 
upper hand, I have more options.</i>

00:12:38.460 --> 00:12:41.440
<i>The more options I have, 
the more opportunities I can create.</i>

00:12:41.770 --> 00:12:44.130
<i>On top of that, it's my serve.</i>

00:12:44.130 --> 00:12:45.670
<i>If I can get my first serve in,</i>

00:12:45.670 --> 00:12:48.720
<i>there's a good chance I can
choose the best option right away.</i>

00:12:48.720 --> 00:12:49.520
<i>All right...</i>

00:12:49.520 --> 00:12:50.470
<i>Here I go!</i>

00:12:54.370 --> 00:12:55.370
<i>Here it comes.</i>

00:12:55.370 --> 00:12:57.610
<i>What do I choose here?</i>

00:12:57.610 --> 00:12:59.520
<i>Araya-kun's probably assuming</i>

00:12:59.520 --> 00:13:02.680
<i>I'll either go for a deep stroke 
or a shallow drop shot.</i>

00:13:03.560 --> 00:13:05.220
<i>Then I'll split the difference,</i>

00:13:05.220 --> 00:13:07.010
<i>and go for a spin that tails out!</i>

00:13:08.250 --> 00:13:11.600
<i>Araya-kun's balance is off because 
he was expecting a short ball.</i>

00:13:11.820 --> 00:13:12.600
<i>Now I can...</i>

00:13:13.770 --> 00:13:15.190
15-love.

00:13:15.190 --> 00:13:16.540
Yes!

00:13:16.540 --> 00:13:18.440
<i>I'm able to disrupt Araya-kun's tempo.</i>

00:13:22.020 --> 00:13:24.320
<i>I can match him even with slower shots.</i>

00:13:24.320 --> 00:13:26.940
<i>If I hit hard now, it'll be a power shot!</i>

00:13:30.140 --> 00:13:31.830
Out. 30-love.

00:13:32.130 --> 00:13:34.570
Wasn't that shot by Maruo incredible?

00:13:34.570 --> 00:13:36.830
Yeah, it looked really fast!

00:13:39.120 --> 00:13:40.990
Araya's really irritated.

00:13:40.990 --> 00:13:42.840
He's always so easy to read.

00:13:43.160 --> 00:13:45.990
Bro is making good use of speed.

00:13:45.990 --> 00:13:48.760
Yeah, even if he can't hit a fast ball,

00:13:48.760 --> 00:13:51.720
he can make it <i>feel</i> like it's fast.

00:13:51.990 --> 00:13:55.140
And when facing an opponent who's 
strengths are speed and power,

00:13:55.140 --> 00:13:57.350
changing your pace is an effective strategy.

00:13:57.610 --> 00:14:01.160
So switching up speeds is a tactic?

00:14:01.160 --> 00:14:01.980
Right.

00:14:02.230 --> 00:14:06.610
Generally, it's easy to get into the 
rhythm if the same pace continues,

00:14:06.610 --> 00:14:09.830
so matches often settle into a steady pace.

00:14:09.830 --> 00:14:13.030
Altering that pace will disrupt 
your opponent's rhythm.

00:14:13.510 --> 00:14:17.440
You can take control by switching 
up your speeds and shots.

00:14:17.980 --> 00:14:20.210
Maruo's shots are average in terms of power,

00:14:20.210 --> 00:14:21.780
but he has great control.

00:14:21.780 --> 00:14:25.420
Players like that are best 
suited to use this strategy.

00:14:25.860 --> 00:14:29.390
If his ability to change 
pace becomes even greater,

00:14:29.390 --> 00:14:33.010
he should even be able to face
 the top players in the country.

00:14:33.010 --> 00:14:36.000
After all, this is the essence of his mantra,

00:14:36.000 --> 00:14:41.480
"If you can return all shots with excellent
control, theoretically, you will never lose."

00:14:41.780 --> 00:14:43.920
<i>30-love in a serve game.</i>

00:14:44.680 --> 00:14:46.360
<i>I should give it my all here and...</i>

00:14:48.960 --> 00:14:50.250
<i>It didn't go in.</i>

00:14:50.250 --> 00:14:52.860
<i>But it's okay as long as I get 
70% of my first serves in.</i>

00:14:52.860 --> 00:14:53.990
40-love.

00:14:53.990 --> 00:14:54.370
Huh?

00:14:55.370 --> 00:14:56.950
Did that go in just now?

00:14:56.950 --> 00:14:58.680
Uh, I couldn't tell.

00:14:58.680 --> 00:15:00.000
Wasn't that a fault?

00:15:00.000 --> 00:15:02.670
Hey, that was in?

00:15:02.670 --> 00:15:04.670
Y-Yes...

00:15:04.670 --> 00:15:06.450
<i>That felt like a fault.</i>

00:15:06.450 --> 00:15:09.300
<i>But that's for the ref to decide.</i>

00:15:09.510 --> 00:15:11.530
<i>Are you kidding me?!</i>

00:15:11.530 --> 00:15:13.610
<i>...is what I want to say.</i>

00:15:14.030 --> 00:15:16.170
<i>But after facing him last year, I learned...</i>

00:15:17.770 --> 00:15:19.330
<i>Anger doesn't help.</i>

00:15:20.080 --> 00:15:23.340
<i>Anger can momentarily change the situation</i>

00:15:23.340 --> 00:15:26.620
<i>if you take it out on something or someone.</i>

00:15:26.620 --> 00:15:29.110
<i>But in the end, it warps you.</i>

00:15:40.580 --> 00:15:43.650
<i>Release the anger unto the sky.</i>

00:15:47.720 --> 00:15:49.510
<i>His expression changed.</i>

00:15:49.950 --> 00:15:52.760
<i>While Araya-kun is trying to recover is when</i>

00:15:52.760 --> 00:15:53.770
<i>I should strike!</i>

00:15:55.940 --> 00:15:57.760
Game: Maruo. 3-0.

00:15:57.990 --> 00:15:59.190
Second set.

00:15:59.190 --> 00:16:01.280
Maruo's still holding the lead.

00:16:02.200 --> 00:16:04.410
<i>After increasing the number
of options for attacks,</i>

00:16:04.410 --> 00:16:06.060
<i>I've had some leverage.</i>

00:16:06.990 --> 00:16:10.440
<i>But it won't be so easy from here on.</i>

00:16:10.790 --> 00:16:14.100
<i>Even if Araya-kun doesn't base 
his play on strategies or skills,</i>

00:16:14.100 --> 00:16:17.080
<i>he can change his play by 
altering his mental state.</i>

00:16:18.110 --> 00:16:19.840
<i>Our last match was like that, too.</i>

00:16:20.290 --> 00:16:23.000
<i>I have to be extremely cautious of 
Araya-kun after he causes a scene.</i>

00:16:23.560 --> 00:16:26.250
<i>People who can play while 
expressing their emotions</i>

00:16:26.250 --> 00:16:29.050
<i>always surpass what I expect of them.</i>

00:16:29.450 --> 00:16:30.100
Time.

00:16:30.560 --> 00:16:34.050
<i>At any rate, Araya-kun will be 
playing harder from here on.</i>

00:16:34.290 --> 00:16:37.800
<i>And if I can't surpass him
when he's like that, I can't win.</i>

00:16:43.880 --> 00:16:44.870
<i>Concentrate.</i>

00:16:48.030 --> 00:16:49.020
15-love.

00:16:49.600 --> 00:16:50.570
There's the ace!

00:16:51.290 --> 00:16:53.610
<i>I can't do anything about those, 
so I'll switch gears.</i>

00:16:55.860 --> 00:16:56.910
30-love.

00:16:56.910 --> 00:16:58.400
Two aces in a row!

00:16:58.400 --> 00:17:00.330
Araya-senpai! Nice serve!

00:17:00.330 --> 00:17:02.160
<i>To think he improved the accuracy</i>

00:17:02.160 --> 00:17:04.830
<i>of his flat serve this much 
with just that one yell.</i>

00:17:04.830 --> 00:17:08.840
<i>I was finally able to figure out how
to deal with the power and speed</i>

00:17:08.840 --> 00:17:12.030
<i>of his left-handed shots 
and gain the upper hand.</i>

00:17:12.030 --> 00:17:14.340
<i>I won't let him just flip that 
around on me so easily!</i>

00:17:16.500 --> 00:17:17.840
Wow, nice return!

00:17:18.600 --> 00:17:20.220
<i>With the advantage I have now,</i>

00:17:20.220 --> 00:17:22.350
<i>I can go with my 1/64 control!</i>

00:17:24.030 --> 00:17:25.980
Out. 30-15.

00:17:25.980 --> 00:17:27.100
Yes!

00:17:28.000 --> 00:17:30.010
<i>Even if Araya-kun's on 
a roll while it's his serve,</i>

00:17:30.010 --> 00:17:32.610
<i>I can gain the advantage 
if I overcome the risks.</i>

00:17:32.610 --> 00:17:35.490
What? He went back to his
original return position.

00:17:35.810 --> 00:17:37.320
<i>You sure about that?</i>

00:17:37.690 --> 00:17:40.660
<i>It's dangerous to split the center 
when the serves are good.</i>

00:17:40.660 --> 00:17:44.830
<i>And if I return them, Araya-kun 
will definitely go for...</i>

00:17:44.830 --> 00:17:46.330
<i>a wide slice!</i>

00:17:46.330 --> 00:17:47.680
<i>My swing was too late!</i>

00:17:49.230 --> 00:17:50.580
40-15.

00:17:51.250 --> 00:17:53.090
<i>It's no use, even if I'm aware of it.</i>

00:17:57.080 --> 00:17:58.820
Game: Araya. 3-1.

00:17:58.820 --> 00:17:59.760
Second set.

00:18:00.010 --> 00:18:01.760
Yeah!

00:18:02.850 --> 00:18:04.930
<i>I guess breaking twice in a row was impossible.</i>

00:18:04.930 --> 00:18:09.940
Araya
Maruo

00:18:05.350 --> 00:18:07.200
<i>But the score is 3-1.</i>

00:18:07.200 --> 00:18:09.930
<i>I can win this set if I can protect my lead.</i>

00:18:09.930 --> 00:18:11.440
<i>No matter what...</i>

00:18:11.440 --> 00:18:12.440
<i>I have to hold!</i>

00:18:13.700 --> 00:18:15.570
<i>After gaining the advantage, I choose...</i>

00:18:15.810 --> 00:18:18.070
<i>A high-arching shot deep to his backhand!</i>

00:18:21.490 --> 00:18:22.990
<i>He's taking smaller steps than usual!</i>

00:18:23.960 --> 00:18:24.990
Love-15.

00:18:25.670 --> 00:18:26.930
<i>He got me!</i>

00:18:26.930 --> 00:18:29.280
Yeah! Bring it!

00:18:29.280 --> 00:18:33.170
<i>No, he's just adjusting to the change 
in pace faster than I expected.</i>

00:18:33.170 --> 00:18:37.890
Araya-kun plays rough, but maybe 
the reason he's good at adjusting

00:18:37.890 --> 00:18:41.920
is that he's always been 
second best to Takuma.

00:18:41.920 --> 00:18:42.970
True...

00:18:43.180 --> 00:18:46.770
Maybe the more you lose, the easier 
it is to accept and learn from it.

00:18:47.530 --> 00:18:50.760
<i>Instead of going for a slow 
ball with a fast-paced hit,</i>

00:18:50.760 --> 00:18:52.880
<i>he matched my timing.</i>

00:18:52.880 --> 00:18:56.780
<i>If he's able to do that, using too 
many slow shots is dangerous.</i>

00:19:00.180 --> 00:19:02.900
<i>If I focus on fast balls, 
when should I change the pace,</i>

00:19:02.900 --> 00:19:04.910
<i>and what should I break down to attack?</i>

00:19:05.830 --> 00:19:06.730
<i>Maruo!</i>

00:19:06.730 --> 00:19:08.780
<i>You're the best opponent I've ever had.</i>

00:19:08.780 --> 00:19:11.090
<i>Thanks to you, I can fix my weaknesses!</i>

00:19:11.090 --> 00:19:13.790
<i>I'll show you where your limit is!</i>

00:19:15.810 --> 00:19:18.170
<i>I tried with a slower hit 
last time, but this time...</i>

00:19:18.720 --> 00:19:19.640
<i>Fast ball!</i>

00:19:20.680 --> 00:19:22.680
<i>After he collapses his stance, 
aim for the open court!</i>

00:19:23.930 --> 00:19:24.970
15-all.

00:19:25.420 --> 00:19:29.060
<i>Yes! That slow shot earlier worked here!</i>

00:19:31.360 --> 00:19:32.560
<i>Nat-chan!</i>

00:19:33.470 --> 00:19:34.690
<i>Her match is over.</i>

00:19:35.190 --> 00:19:36.940
<i>Nat-chan won!</i>

00:19:40.870 --> 00:19:42.200
Takasaki-san!

00:19:42.860 --> 00:19:44.200
I won!

00:19:44.200 --> 00:19:45.240
That's amazing!

00:19:45.240 --> 00:19:47.610
Congratulations on the win, Nat-chan!

00:19:47.610 --> 00:19:50.000
Congrats! That was fast.

00:19:50.000 --> 00:19:51.210
Thanks!

00:19:51.890 --> 00:19:54.330
So Ei-chan is leading in the second set?

00:19:54.330 --> 00:19:56.810
He just made his first break.

00:19:56.810 --> 00:19:58.970
He changed the tempo by mixing up his shots.

00:19:58.970 --> 00:20:00.980
It was a very Ei-chan-like play.

00:20:00.980 --> 00:20:03.930
So he's able to change the pace well, huh?

00:20:11.270 --> 00:20:13.260
Game: Maruo. 4-1.

00:20:13.260 --> 00:20:14.400
Second set!

00:20:14.400 --> 00:20:16.240
<i>Yes, I held!</i>

00:20:16.240 --> 00:20:19.300
<i>But I've still broken only once.</i>

00:20:19.940 --> 00:20:21.440
Araya 2-4 Maruo

00:20:21.440 --> 00:20:22.950
Araya 2-5 Maruo

00:20:22.950 --> 00:20:24.450
Araya 3-5 Maruo

00:20:24.450 --> 00:20:25.950
You can do it, Ei-chan!

00:20:25.950 --> 00:20:27.200
If you win this game...

00:20:28.420 --> 00:20:29.770
<i>Set point!</i>

00:20:29.770 --> 00:20:31.700
<i>If I win this, we'll be going to the final set!</i>

00:20:31.700 --> 00:20:32.600
<i>I'll go for...</i>

00:20:32.600 --> 00:20:33.710
Another drop shot!

00:20:35.830 --> 00:20:37.490
He's already over there!

00:20:37.490 --> 00:20:38.340
That's so risky!

00:20:39.150 --> 00:20:40.090
He got it!

00:20:41.590 --> 00:20:43.760
Second set winner: Maruo.

00:20:43.760 --> 00:20:45.300
Score: 6-3.

00:20:45.980 --> 00:20:47.020
Yes!

00:20:47.850 --> 00:20:51.290
Amazing! He took the set from Araya!

00:20:51.290 --> 00:20:55.410
Seriously? Wasn't Araya ranked in
the nation's top eight last year?

00:20:55.410 --> 00:20:58.480
Well, he only won the set. 
We don't know how it'll end!

00:20:58.830 --> 00:21:00.660
This means they're tied, right?

00:21:00.660 --> 00:21:03.610
Yeah! Whoever gets the next set wins!

00:21:04.060 --> 00:21:06.100
<i>It's curious, though...</i>

00:21:06.100 --> 00:21:08.830
<i>Araya's reactions are gradually improving.</i>

00:21:13.360 --> 00:21:17.180
<i>My moves are getting better 
every time I return his drop shots.</i>

00:21:17.860 --> 00:21:21.400
<i>Maybe it's because it reminds 
me of facing Takuma.</i>

00:21:21.880 --> 00:21:23.920
<i>Even though it's the prefectural final,</i>

00:21:23.920 --> 00:21:28.150
<i>I honestly didn't think my engine would 
start since I'm not facing Takuma.</i>

00:21:28.610 --> 00:21:32.880
<i>I could never admit that to 
my teammates who left...</i>

00:21:33.300 --> 00:21:34.850
<i>But I honestly couldn't do it.</i>

00:21:36.350 --> 00:21:38.040
<i>But this match reminded me</i>

00:21:38.040 --> 00:21:40.570
<i>how I felt when I first started playing tennis.</i>

00:21:40.990 --> 00:21:43.160
<i>Playing you, not Takuma, in this final round</i>

00:21:43.880 --> 00:21:45.960
<i>made me remember that.</i>

00:21:47.180 --> 00:21:50.250
<i>In the end, I'm not doing this for someone,</i>

00:21:50.250 --> 00:21:52.970
<i>or for honor, or anything like that.</i>

00:21:53.480 --> 00:21:56.830
<i>I just hate to lose!</i>

00:21:57.060 --> 00:21:59.780
I hope Ei-chan can keep this up and win.

00:21:59.780 --> 00:22:01.560
I'm not so sure about that.

00:22:01.560 --> 00:22:05.720
Not only does Araya have explosive power,
but he's playing better and better, too.

00:22:05.720 --> 00:22:08.000
On top of that, he has more 
experience than Maruo,

00:22:08.000 --> 00:22:10.330
so he knows how to deal 
with a critical close contest.

00:22:10.760 --> 00:22:14.720
The final set will be a rough 
battle, no matter what.

00:22:14.950 --> 00:22:17.230
Still, I think he'll be fine.

00:22:19.090 --> 00:22:22.160
Because Ei-chan looks like he's having fun!

00:22:23.200 --> 00:22:23.830
Time!

00:22:29.300 --> 00:22:31.990
<i>It's time! The final set!</i>

00:24:00.000 --> 00:24:25.020
Next Time

00:24:00.560 --> 00:24:02.920
Araya-kun is on his game, 
which is dangerous for me!

00:24:03.290 --> 00:24:05.210
I need to learn to trust my instincts more.

00:24:05.210 --> 00:24:07.090
But for now, I'll trust my reason!

00:24:07.090 --> 00:24:09.470
After playing against you 
for this long, I can tell...

00:24:09.470 --> 00:24:11.750
You want to go pro, don't you?

00:24:11.750 --> 00:24:13.630
All the more reason I can't let you win.

00:24:13.840 --> 00:24:15.380
No matter when you change your pace,

00:24:15.380 --> 00:24:17.080
no matter where you control your shots.

00:24:17.080 --> 00:24:19.390
In the end, my legs will catch up to you!

00:24:19.950 --> 00:24:22.020
Next time on <i>Baby Steps</i>,

00:24:21.540 --> 00:24:25.020
Full Force Toward Adversity

00:24:22.020 --> 00:24:23.430
"Full Force Toward Adversity."

00:24:25.210 --> 00:24:30.350
End
